{"id": "sdqa0077", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 5.4, "n_chunks": 6, "n_concurrent": 6, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.0989, "eot_read_ms": 18, "reference_answer": "Speed describes only how fast an object is moving, whereas velocity gives both how fast it is and in which direction the object is moving", "query": "What is the difference between speed and velocity?", "transcript": "What is the difference between speed and velocity?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Speed is a scalar quantity, so it only tells you how fast something is moving without any direction. Velocity, on the other hand, is a vector quantity and includes both the speed and the direction of movement. For example, if you say \"60 km/h,\" that's just speed. But if you say \"60 km/h north,\" that's velocity because it specifies the direction as well.", "expert_answer": "Speed is a **scalar** quantity: it tells how fast something is moving, with no direction.\n\nVelocity is a **vector** quantity: it tells how fast something is moving **and in what direction**.\n\nExample:  \n- Speed: **60 km/h**  \n- Velocity: **60 km/h north**", "expert_latency_s": 1.6, "stall_ms": 20, "relay_ms": 1703}
