{"id": "sdqa0104", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 3.8, "n_chunks": 4, "n_concurrent": 4, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1903, "eot_read_ms": 17, "reference_answer": "Motor tics are movement-based tics affecting discrete muscle groups", "query": "What is a motor tic?", "transcript": "What is a motor tick?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "A motor tic is a sudden, brief movement caused by involuntary muscle contractions. These can be simple, like a twitch or blink, or more complex involving several movements at once. They're often linked to conditions like Tourette syndrome but can also happen occasionally in healthy people.", "expert_answer": "A **motor tic** (often misspelled “motor tick”) is a **sudden, rapid, repetitive, non-rhythmic movement** caused by involuntary or semi-voluntary muscle contractions.\n\nExamples include:\n- Eye blinking\n- Facial grimacing\n- Shoulder shrugging\n- Head jerking\n- Nose twitching\n\nMotor tics can be **simple** (one brief movement, like blinking) or **complex** (a sequence of movements, like touching objects or jumping). They are commonly associated with tic disorders, including **Tourette syndrome**, but can also occur transiently, especially in children.", "expert_latency_s": 3.54, "stall_ms": 19, "relay_ms": 1146}
